-- New-style daily words for March (days 60–90): a real devotion (paragraphs ending "Today: …"), a declaration and a prayer. Same KJV verse as before.
-- Only replaces days still in the old one-paragraph style, so anything rewritten in Admin → Devotions is kept.

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'In the Sermon on the Mount, Jesus warned about people who prayed loudly on street corners so others would admire them. They had their reward: the applause of people. Then He said: "But thou, when thou prayest, enter into thy closet, and when thou hast shut thy door, pray to thy Father which is in secret."

The word "closet" meant an inner room, often the storeroom of the house: a small, private place with no audience.

Public prayer is good. Jesus prayed with others, and the church prays together. But the root of our prayer life must be private, where nobody is watching and nobody is impressed. That is where we find out whether we are praying to God or performing for people.

Your secret place does not have to be special. It might be your bedroom before the house wakes up, a quiet corner at work during lunch, or your car before you go into the office. Some mothers have made a "closet" by putting a cloth over their head in a busy house.

The promise is beautiful: "thy Father which seeth in secret shall reward thee openly." The prayers no one else hears are heard by the One who matters most.

Today: choose a secret place and a time. Go there, shut the door, even if it is only the door of your heart, and talk to your Father for a few honest minutes.',
  declaration = 'I meet my Father in the secret place. He sees me there, hears me and rewards me.',
  prayer = 'Father, I do not want my prayers to be a performance. Draw me into the secret place with You. Meet me there, hear my honest words and shape my life from that hidden time with You. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy060' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'James was writing to believers who were fighting among themselves, chasing pleasures and trying to be friends with the world while also following God. He calls them "double minded": trying to walk two roads at the same time.

In the middle of this hard rebuke comes a wonderful invitation: "Draw nigh to God, and he will draw nigh to you."

God is not hiding from us or waiting for us to become perfect. The moment we turn toward Him, He comes near. Like the father of the prodigal son, He is already looking down the road.

But James also says: "Cleanse your hands, ye sinners; and purify your hearts, ye double minded." Drawing near is not just a warm feeling. It involves turning from what pulls us away: cleansing our hands from wrong actions and our hearts from divided loyalty.

Think about two people who want to sit together but keep a bag between them on the bench. Before they can be close, the bag has to move. Sometimes there are things in our lives that sit between us and God.

The good news is that God does not ask us to clean ourselves up first and then come. We draw near, and in His presence He helps us let go.

Today: take one step toward God: a short prayer, a few verses, a song. And ask Him to show you one thing you need to put down so you can come closer.',
  declaration = 'As I draw near to God, He draws near to me. I put away what divides my heart.',
  prayer = 'Lord, I want to be close to You. Forgive my divided heart. Show me what I need to put down, and give me the courage to let it go. Thank You that as I draw near to You, You draw near to me. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy061' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Joel prophesied after a terrible plague of locusts. Fields were stripped bare, vines and fig trees destroyed, and people had nothing left to offer even in the temple. Joel called the nation to repent, and God spoke this tender invitation: "turn ye even to me with all your heart, and with fasting, and with weeping, and with mourning: And rend your heart, and not your garments, and turn unto the LORD your God."

In those days, people tore their clothes to show grief or repentance. It was a dramatic outward sign. But God said: I am not interested in torn clothes if your heart is untouched. Tear your heart.

It is possible to do many religious things outwardly: fast, cry in church, raise our hands, wear modest clothes, while our hearts remain hard. God looks past the outward show to the heart.

Then Joel gives the reason we can return: God "is gracious and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness." We do not turn back to a cruel master, but to a merciful Father.

Many churches use the weeks before Easter as a season of turning back to God with prayer and fasting. Whatever your tradition, this is a good season to let God touch your heart, not just your habits.

Today: ask God, "Where have I gone through the motions without my heart?" Tell Him honestly, and turn back to Him with your whole heart.',
  declaration = 'I return to the Lord with all my heart. He is gracious and merciful, slow to anger and full of kindness.',
  prayer = 'Lord, I do not want an outward religion. Break my heart where it has become hard and proud. I turn back to You with all my heart, trusting Your grace and mercy. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy062' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'David wrote Psalm 51 after the prophet Nathan confronted him about his sin with Bathsheba and the murder of her husband Uriah. The king who had written so many beautiful psalms had fallen terribly. And here he does not make excuses. He asks God for something only God can do: "Create in me a clean heart, O God; and renew a right spirit within me."

The word "create" is the same word used in Genesis 1, when God created the heavens and the earth. David knew he could not fix himself. He did not just need a cleaning. He needed a new creation.

He also asked God to "renew a right spirit", a steadfast spirit that would not wander so easily again.

Some of us try to deal with sin by promising to do better, managing it ourselves, or hiding it. David''s way is better: honest confession and asking God to change the heart itself.

Psalm 51 also shows that sin is not the end of the story for those who turn to God. David asks God to restore the joy of his salvation, and he promises to teach others God''s ways. God''s grace turns broken people into witnesses of His mercy.

Today: pray this verse slowly, three times. Each time, think about one area of your heart you want God to create anew.',
  declaration = 'God creates in me a clean heart and renews a right spirit within me.',
  prayer = 'Create in me a clean heart, O God, and renew a right spirit within me. I cannot fix myself. Wash me, restore the joy of Your salvation, and make me a living witness of Your mercy. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy063' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Before Israel entered the promised land, Moses warned them that if they turned to idols, they would be scattered among the nations. It was a sad prediction, and it came true years later. But even then, he said: "if from thence thou shalt seek the LORD thy God, thou shalt find him, if thou seek him with all thy heart and with all thy soul."

"From thence": from exile, from the consequences of their own wrong choices, from the far country. Even there, God could be found.

Maybe you feel you are far from God because of choices you made. You moved away from Him slowly, or rebelled openly. Now you are living with the results, and you wonder whether God will still receive you. This verse says: yes, even from there.

But notice the condition: "with all thy heart and with all thy soul." God is found by those who seek Him wholeheartedly, not casually or with half of their attention.

Wholehearted seeking might look like setting aside other things to pray, opening your Bible when you would rather scroll, confessing honestly, asking for help from mature believers, or fasting from something that distracts you.

God is not hiding from those who truly look for Him. He wants to be found.

Today: wherever you are spiritually, seek God from there. Spend focused time with Him, with all your heart, and trust His promise that you will find Him.',
  declaration = 'Wherever I am, if I seek the Lord with all my heart and soul, I will find Him.',
  prayer = 'Lord, I am seeking You from where I am today, even with my mistakes and distance. Help me seek You with all my heart and soul. Thank You that You promise to be found. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy064' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Jesus told the story of a younger son who demanded his inheritance, left home and wasted everything. When famine came, he ended up feeding pigs and wishing he could eat their food. Finally he "came to himself" and decided to go home and ask to be a servant.

Then comes this verse: "when he was yet a great way off, his father saw him, and had compassion, and ran, and fell on his neck, and kissed him."

The father saw him from far away. He had been watching the road. He ran. In that culture, an older man running in public was undignified. But this father did not care about his dignity. He cared about his son.

The son had a speech prepared, but the father interrupted it with a robe, a ring, shoes and a feast. He did not make his son earn his way back.

This is how Jesus described God: a Father who watches for His children, who runs toward them, who welcomes them home with joy.

Many of us imagine God standing at the gate with folded arms, waiting for us to grovel. But Jesus shows us a Father running down the road.

Today: if you have been far from God, take the first step home. Tell Him honestly where you have been, and receive His welcome. If you are already home, ask Him to give you His heart for those who are still far away.',
  declaration = 'My Father sees me from afar, runs to me with compassion and welcomes me home.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You for watching the road for me. Thank You for running to me with compassion when I turn back. Help me receive Your welcome, and give me Your heart for those who are still far away. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy065' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'The letter to the Hebrews was written to believers who were tempted to give up on Jesus and go back to their old ways. The writer reminds them that Jesus is our great High Priest, and in the verse just before this one he says: "we have not an high priest which cannot be touched with the feeling of our infirmities; but was in all points tempted like as we are, yet without sin."

Jesus understands. He knows hunger, tiredness, rejection, grief, betrayal and temptation. He is not a distant ruler who cannot relate to us.

Therefore: "Let us therefore come boldly unto the throne of grace."

In the Old Testament, the high priest could enter the Most Holy Place only once a year, with fear and trembling. Now, because of Jesus, we can come anytime. And the throne we approach is not a throne of judgment for us, but a throne of grace.

We come to "obtain mercy" for our failures and to "find grace to help in time of need" for our weaknesses. Mercy deals with the past. Grace helps us for today and tomorrow.

Coming boldly does not mean coming proudly. It means coming confidently, not because we deserve it, but because Jesus has opened the way.

Today: whatever your need is, come to God boldly in prayer. Name it clearly. Ask for mercy where you have failed and grace where you are weak.',
  declaration = 'I come boldly to the throne of grace. I receive mercy for my failures and grace to help in my time of need.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You that Jesus understands my weakness. I come boldly to Your throne of grace, not because I deserve it, but because of Him. Give me mercy for my failures and grace for what I am facing today. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy066' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Peter wrote these words to believers who were suffering. Some were being insulted, others mistreated, and the elders were carrying heavy responsibilities. He told them: "Humble yourselves therefore under the mighty hand of God, that he may exalt you in due time: Casting all your care upon him; for he careth for you."

Humility and anxiety are connected. Pride says, "I must carry everything myself. Everything depends on me." Humility says, "God is mighty, and I am not. I will put myself under His hand."

The word "casting" is the same word used when the disciples threw their cloaks on the donkey''s colt before Jesus rode into Jerusalem. You do not gently place your worries on God. You throw them onto Him.

Imagine a man carrying a heavy bag of cement up a long staircase. A strong friend at the top calls, "Throw it to me!" Pride says, "I can manage." Humility throws it.

And the reason is simple: "he careth for you." God is not annoyed by your worries. He is not too busy. He cares.

Notice also "in due time". God''s lifting comes at His time, not ours. Humility includes trusting His timing.

Today: write down your main worries. For each one, pray: "Lord, I throw this onto You because You care for me." Then leave it there.',
  declaration = 'I humble myself under God''s mighty hand. I cast all my cares on Him, because He cares for me.',
  prayer = 'Lord, I humble myself under Your mighty hand. I am tired of carrying everything myself. I throw my worries onto You, because You care for me. Lift me up in Your time. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy067' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Jesus spoke these words to ordinary people who were worn out: by hard work, by poverty, by the heavy religious rules their leaders loaded on them. "Come unto me, all ye that labour and are heavy laden, and I will give you rest."

Notice who is invited: all who labour and are heavy laden. Not the strong, the successful or the spiritually impressive. The tired.

Then Jesus adds: "Take my yoke upon you, and learn of me; for I am meek and lowly in heart: and ye shall find rest unto your souls." A yoke was a wooden frame placed on oxen so they could pull a plough together. Jewish teachers also spoke of taking on the "yoke" of their teaching.

It seems strange: Jesus offers rest, but also a yoke. But His yoke is different. It is "easy", well-fitted, and His burden is light. Jesus does not remove all work from our lives. He invites us to walk with Him, learning His way, pulling alongside Him instead of alone.

Many people are tired not just in their bodies but in their souls: tired of pretending, tired of striving to earn love, tired of carrying guilt.

Jesus says: come to Me. Learn from Me. I am gentle and humble. You will find rest for your souls.

Today: tell Jesus honestly what is making you weary. Then spend a few quiet minutes simply resting in His presence, without trying to achieve anything.',
  declaration = 'I come to Jesus with my weariness. I take His yoke, learn from Him and find rest for my soul.',
  prayer = 'Lord Jesus, I am tired. I come to You with everything that is weighing me down. Teach me Your gentle and humble way. Let me walk with You and find rest for my soul. In Your name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy068' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Psalm 145 is a song of praise to God the King. David praises His greatness, His goodness and His faithfulness to all generations. Then he says: "The LORD is nigh unto all them that call upon him, to all that call upon him in truth."

The great King of the universe is near. Not only to kings, priests or prophets, but to all who call on Him.

But there is a key phrase: "in truth". God is near to those who call on Him honestly, without pretence.

Many of us struggle to be honest in prayer. We use formal words we think God wants to hear. We hide our anger, doubt or disappointment. But the psalms are full of honest prayers: "How long, O LORD?" "Why standest thou afar off?" God is not offended by honesty. He is near to it.

Calling on Him in truth also means calling on the true God as He really is, not a God we have made up to fit our wishes.

A child who calls for her mother at night does not use polished speeches. She simply calls, honestly and urgently. That is the kind of prayer God draws near to.

Today: take a few minutes to tell God the honest truth about how you feel: your fears, frustrations or hopes. Trust that He is near to you as you call on Him.',
  declaration = 'The Lord is near to me as I call upon Him in truth. I can be honest with Him.',
  prayer = 'Lord, thank You that You are near to all who call on You in truth. Help me drop my masks in prayer. Hear my honest words today, and draw near to me as You promised. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy069' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'After the walls of Jerusalem were rebuilt, the people gathered and Ezra read God''s law to them from morning until midday. As they listened, they understood how far they had fallen from God''s ways, and they began to weep.

But Nehemiah and the leaders told them: do not mourn or weep today. "Go your way, eat the fat, and drink the sweet, and send portions unto them for whom nothing is prepared: for this day is holy unto our Lord: neither be ye sorry; for the joy of the LORD is your strength."

Conviction is good. God''s Word should touch us and lead us to repent. But God does not want us to stay in endless sorrow. After confession comes celebration, because God forgives and restores.

"The joy of the LORD" is not joy in our circumstances. It is joy in who God is and what He has done. That joy becomes strength: strength to keep going, to resist temptation, to serve others, to face hard days.

Notice also that the celebration was not selfish. They were to "send portions" to those who had nothing prepared. Joy in God makes us generous.

Today: if you have been feeling heavy with guilt or discouragement, receive God''s forgiveness and let His joy lift you. Then share your joy practically by helping someone who has little.',
  declaration = 'The joy of the Lord is my strength. After repentance comes rejoicing, and His joy makes me generous.',
  prayer = 'Lord, thank You for Your Word that searches my heart, and for Your forgiveness when I repent. Lift my sorrow and fill me with Your joy. Let that joy be my strength and overflow in generosity to others. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy070' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Hosea lived during a time when Israel kept turning away from God to idols and political alliances. God allowed hardship to come as a result. In chapter 6, the people say: "Come, and let us return unto the LORD: for he hath torn, and he will heal us; he hath smitten, and he will bind us up."

They recognise that God allowed the wounds, but they also trust that He will heal. Like a doctor who must sometimes cut in order to heal, God''s discipline is never meant to destroy His children, but to bring them back.

Sadly, the following verses show that Israel''s repentance was shallow, like morning mist that quickly disappears. Their words were good, but their hearts did not change. God wants more than quick words. He wants real return.

Some painful seasons in our lives come from our own choices. Others come from living in a broken world. Either way, God invites us to come to Him for healing. He is not only the God who allows wounds; He is the God who binds them up.

The key is "let us return". Healing begins when we turn toward Him, not when we run further away in shame or anger.

Today: if you are in a painful season, ask God what He wants to teach you through it. Then return to Him honestly, trusting that He will bind up your wounds.',
  declaration = 'I return to the Lord. Though I have been wounded, He will heal me and bind me up.',
  prayer = 'Lord, I bring You my wounds, including the ones I caused myself. I do not want a shallow repentance. I return to You with my whole heart. Heal me, bind me up and make me whole. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy071' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Romans 12 begins with the word "therefore". For eleven chapters, Paul has explained the mercy of God: our sin, Christ''s sacrifice, our justification, the Spirit''s work, God''s faithful plan. Now he says: in light of all this mercy, here is how to respond.

"I beseech you therefore, brethren, by the mercies of God, that ye present your bodies a living sacrifice, holy, acceptable unto God, which is your reasonable service."

In the Old Testament, sacrifices were animals that died on the altar. Paul calls us to be living sacrifices: to offer our whole selves to God every day, while we are alive.

"Your bodies" means more than our spiritual feelings. It includes our eyes, hands, feet, tongues, time and energy. Where we go, what we watch, how we work, what we say.

The difficulty with living sacrifices, someone once said, is that they keep crawling off the altar. We give ourselves to God on Sunday, and by Monday we take ourselves back. That is why this is a daily offering.

And it is "reasonable service". Given everything God has done for us, offering our lives to Him is the most reasonable response.

Today: when you wake up tomorrow, before you get out of bed, offer your body to God: "Lord, my eyes, my hands, my feet, my mouth and my time are Yours today."',
  declaration = 'Because of God''s mercy, I offer my body to Him as a living sacrifice, holy and pleasing to Him.',
  prayer = 'Father, in view of all Your mercy, I offer You my whole self today. My eyes, hands, feet, mouth, time and energy belong to You. Help me live as a living sacrifice, holy and pleasing to You. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy072' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Paul wrote this from prison, not knowing whether he would live or die. Yet he told the Philippians: "Be careful for nothing; but in every thing by prayer and supplication with thanksgiving let your requests be made known unto God."

"Be careful for nothing" means do not be anxious about anything. Paul is not saying problems are unreal. He is showing what to do with them: turn every worry into prayer.

He mentions prayer, supplication and thanksgiving. Prayer is talking with God. Supplication is asking specifically and earnestly. Thanksgiving is remembering His goodness even while we ask. Thanksgiving changes the way we pray; it reminds us that God has been faithful before.

Then comes the promise: "the peace of God, which passeth all understanding, shall keep your hearts and minds through Christ Jesus." The word "keep" was a military word for a garrison guarding a city. God''s peace stands guard over our hearts and minds.

Notice that Paul does not promise we will always get exactly what we ask for. He promises peace: a peace that does not make sense in the situation, but is real.

Today: write down every worry on your mind. Next to each one, write a short prayer and one thing you are thankful for. Then hand the list to God and ask Him to guard your heart with His peace.',
  declaration = 'I am not anxious about anything. I bring everything to God with thanksgiving, and His peace guards my heart and mind.',
  prayer = 'Father, I bring You all my worries today. Here are my requests, and here is my thanks for Your faithfulness. Let Your peace, which passes understanding, guard my heart and mind in Christ Jesus. In His name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy073' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Isaiah 55 begins with a market call: "Ho, every one that thirsteth, come ye to the waters, and he that hath no money; come ye, buy, and eat." God invites people to receive what truly satisfies, freely, without price. Then comes the urgent call: "Seek ye the LORD while he may be found, call ye upon him while he is near."

This verse has a sense of timing. There are seasons when God is especially near, when our hearts are tender and His invitation is strong. In those moments, we should respond.

Think of a hospital visit. A relative might lie in bed hoping to see you, and you keep postponing: "Next week, when I am less busy." Sometimes next week is too late. God''s mercy is great, but we should not keep postponing our response to Him.

The next verse says: "Let the wicked forsake his way... and let him return unto the LORD, and he will have mercy upon him; and to our God, for he will abundantly pardon." The call to seek God is full of hope. God abundantly pardons.

Maybe you have sensed God drawing you lately: to pray, to forgive, to give up a habit, to return to church, to start reading His Word. Do not wait for a perfect moment. Seek Him now, while He may be found.

Today: respond to the most recent nudge you have felt from God. Do it today, not someday.',
  declaration = 'I seek the Lord while He may be found. I call on Him while He is near, and He abundantly pardons.',
  prayer = 'Lord, thank You for drawing near to me. I do not want to delay any longer. I seek You now. Forgive me, and help me respond to every nudge of Your Spirit. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy074' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Psalm 46 is a song about God''s strength in the middle of chaos. It speaks of the earth shaking, mountains falling into the sea, waters roaring, nations raging and kingdoms falling. And in the middle of it all, God says: "Be still, and know that I am God."

"Be still" here means something like "cease striving" or "let go". It is a command to stop fighting, panicking and trying to control everything, and to recognise who God is.

The psalm reminds us that "God is our refuge and strength, a very present help in trouble." Present means right here, in the trouble, not far away.

We live in a noisy world: traffic, generators, phones, news, opinions, arguments. Even our inner world can be noisy with worries and plans. Stillness feels uncomfortable. We would rather keep busy.

But some things can only be learned in stillness. When we stop striving, we begin to see that God is still God. He is still on the throne. He has not lost control.

This does not mean we never act. It means we stop acting out of panic and start acting out of trust.

Today: set a timer for five minutes. Sit quietly, breathe slowly and repeat: "Be still, and know that I am God." When your mind wanders, gently bring it back. Let God be God.',
  declaration = 'I am still, and I know that He is God. He is my refuge and strength, a very present help in trouble.',
  prayer = 'Lord, I am tired of striving and panicking. Quiet my heart. Help me be still and know that You are God. You are my refuge and strength, and I trust You with what I cannot control. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy075' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'In the Sermon on the Mount, Jesus talked about three spiritual practices: giving, praying and fasting. Notice He said "when", not "if". He assumed His followers would fast.

But He warned about the hypocrites who made themselves look miserable so everyone would know they were fasting. So He said: "But thou, when thou fastest, anoint thine head, and wash thy face; That thou appear not unto men to fast, but unto thy Father which is in secret."

Fasting is not a hunger strike to force God''s hand, or a way to impress people with our spirituality. It is a way of saying to God: You matter more to me than food. Every hunger pang becomes a reminder to pray.

Jesus wanted fasting to be private and genuine. Wash your face, look normal, keep it between you and God. That does not mean churches cannot fast together; the early church did. It means our motive is God, not an audience.

If you have a health condition, are pregnant or nursing, or take medication, speak to a doctor before fasting from food. You can also fast from other things: social media, TV or a favourite habit, and use that time to pray.

Today: consider a simple fast this week, even one meal or one evening without your phone. Use the time to pray. Keep it quiet, between you and your Father.',
  declaration = 'When I fast, I fast for my Father, not for an audience. He sees in secret and rewards openly.',
  prayer = 'Father, I want You more than food, entertainment or comfort. Teach me to fast with a pure heart. Use every hunger to draw me to prayer, and meet me in the secret place. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy076' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Just before this verse, Jesus told a story about a man who went to his friend at midnight to borrow bread for an unexpected visitor. The friend was already in bed with his children and did not want to get up. But because the man kept knocking boldly, the friend finally got up and gave him what he needed.

Then Jesus said: "Ask, and it shall be given you; seek, and ye shall find; knock, and it shall be opened unto you."

The point is not that God is a sleepy friend who needs to be pestered. Jesus goes on to compare God with earthly fathers: if you who are imperfect give good gifts to your children, how much more will your heavenly Father give the Holy Spirit to those who ask Him?

The point is persistence and boldness. Keep coming. Do not give up after one prayer.

Notice the gift Jesus highlights: the Holy Spirit. Of all the things we might ask for, the greatest gift is God Himself living in us: His presence, guidance, comfort and power.

Some prayers are answered quickly. Others take years. But every sincere prayer is heard, and our Father gives good gifts, often better than we know how to ask for.

Today: ask your Father for a fresh filling of the Holy Spirit. Then bring one long-standing request to Him again, boldly and persistently.',
  declaration = 'I ask boldly and persistently, because my heavenly Father gives good gifts and the Holy Spirit to those who ask.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You that You love to give good gifts. Fill me afresh with Your Holy Spirit. I bring You again the requests on my heart, and I trust You to answer in Your good way and time. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy077' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'King Solomon had just dedicated the temple in Jerusalem. Fire came down from heaven, and the glory of God filled the house. That night, God appeared to Solomon and gave him this promise: "If my people, which are called by my name, shall humble themselves, and pray, and seek my face, and turn from their wicked ways; then will I hear from heaven, and will forgive their sin, and will heal their land."

Notice who God addresses: "my people". Not the unbelievers or the government first, but His own people. Healing for a land begins with the repentance of God''s people.

There are four steps: humble yourselves, pray, seek His face and turn from wicked ways. Humility admits we need Him. Prayer brings our needs to Him. Seeking His face wants Him, not just His gifts. Turning from wickedness changes how we live.

And three promises follow: God will hear, forgive and heal.

Many believers love to pray this verse over their nation, and they should. Our country needs healing: from corruption, violence, poverty and division. But the verse first calls us to look at ourselves. Are we humble? Are we praying? Are we turning from our own wicked ways?

Today: pray for your nation, its leaders and its people. But begin by asking God to search your own heart, and turn from one wrong way you know about.',
  declaration = 'As God''s people humble themselves, pray, seek His face and turn from wickedness, He will hear, forgive and heal our land.',
  prayer = 'Lord, I humble myself before You. Forgive my sins and the sins of Your people. I pray for my nation and its leaders. Hear from heaven, forgive our sin and heal our land. Start with me. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy078' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Psalm 73 was written by Asaph, a worship leader. He was honest: he had almost slipped in his faith because he envied the wicked. They seemed healthy, rich and carefree, while he tried to live rightly and kept suffering. He wondered whether keeping his heart pure had been worth it.

Then something changed: "Until I went into the sanctuary of God; then understood I their end." In God''s presence, Asaph saw things clearly. The success of the wicked was temporary. God''s presence was what really mattered.

He ends the psalm with this conclusion: "But it is good for me to draw near to God: I have put my trust in the Lord GOD, that I may declare all thy works."

Many believers struggle with the same question. Why does the corrupt official seem to prosper? Why does the dishonest trader build a bigger house? Why does my faithfulness seem to bring more struggle?

Asaph''s answer was not a clever argument. It was an encounter. Drawing near to God put everything in perspective.

He also said, "Whom have I in heaven but thee? and there is none upon earth that I desire beside thee." When God becomes our treasure, envy loses its grip.

Today: if envy or discouragement has been creeping into your heart, go into God''s presence. Worship, read a psalm and say: "It is good for me to draw near to God."',
  declaration = 'It is good for me to draw near to God. I trust in Him, and He is my treasure.',
  prayer = 'Lord, forgive me when I envy others and doubt Your goodness. Draw me into Your presence and help me see clearly. It is good for me to be near You. Be my treasure above everything else. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy079' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Zechariah prophesied to the Jews who had returned from exile in Babylon. They had started rebuilding the temple, but the work had stalled, and their spiritual life had become cold. God''s first message through Zechariah was simple: "Turn ye unto me, saith the LORD of hosts, and I will turn unto you."

God reminded them that their fathers had refused to listen to the earlier prophets, and judgment had come. Now He was giving this generation a choice.

The phrase "LORD of hosts" appears three times in this one verse. It means the Lord of armies, the One who commands the angel armies of heaven. The mighty God was humbly inviting His people back.

Notice that the turning is mutual. When we turn to God, He turns to us. He is not waiting with His back turned, hoping we will fail.

Many people return from a "Babylon" of their own: a season far from God, a backslidden time, a painful experience. They come back, but slowly grow cold again, busy with their own houses while God''s work stands unfinished.

God''s call is the same: turn to Me. Not just to church activity, but to Me.

Today: ask yourself, "Have I turned to God''s work but not to God Himself?" Spend time today turning your heart directly to Him in prayer and worship.',
  declaration = 'I turn to the Lord of hosts, and He turns to me.',
  prayer = 'Lord of hosts, I turn to You again. Forgive me for growing cold and busy with my own things. Turn to me and revive my heart. Let my service flow from love for You. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy080' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Proverbs is full of short sayings that are easy to remember and hard to live. This one is a warning: "When pride cometh, then cometh shame: but with the lowly is wisdom."

Pride does not usually announce itself. It slips in quietly: refusing advice, needing to be right, looking down on others, taking credit for what God has done, being unable to say "I am sorry" or "I do not know".

And shame follows pride like a shadow. The proud person eventually falls, and the fall is often public and painful.

King Uzziah of Judah was a good and successful king. But when he became strong, his heart was lifted up, and he went into the temple to burn incense, which only priests were allowed to do. He became a leper and lived in isolation until he died.

The second half of the verse gives the alternative: "with the lowly is wisdom." Humble people learn. They listen, ask questions, admit mistakes and grow. Wisdom flows easily into a humble heart, like water flowing into a valley rather than onto a mountain top.

Humility is not thinking badly of yourself. It is thinking rightly: seeing God as great, seeing yourself as His dependent child, and seeing others as worthy of respect.

Today: ask someone you trust for honest feedback in one area of your life, and listen without defending yourself. Thank them, and pray over what they said.',
  declaration = 'I choose humility over pride. With the lowly is wisdom, and God gives me wisdom as I humble myself.',
  prayer = 'Lord, search my heart for pride. Forgive me where I have refused correction or looked down on others. Make me humble and teachable, so I can receive Your wisdom. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy081' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'On the night He was betrayed, Jesus went to the Mount of Olives to pray. He knew what was coming: the arrest, the beatings, the cross and, worst of all, carrying the sin of the world. Luke tells us He prayed so earnestly that His sweat was like great drops of blood falling to the ground.

And He prayed: "Father, if thou be willing, remove this cup from me: nevertheless not my will, but thine, be done."

This prayer shows us two things. First, Jesus was honest. He did not pretend the cross was easy. He asked if there was another way. We are allowed to tell God what we want, even our deepest fears.

Second, Jesus surrendered. "Not my will, but thine." His desire was real, but His trust in the Father was deeper.

This is the hardest prayer we ever pray. It is easy to say "Thy will be done" when God''s will matches ours. It is costly when His will means pain, waiting or giving up something precious.

But because Jesus prayed this prayer and drank the cup, we have salvation. His surrender brought resurrection. Our surrenders, too, are never wasted in God''s hands.

Today: name one area where your will and God''s will may be different. Tell Him honestly what you want. Then pray, as Jesus did: "Nevertheless, not my will, but Yours be done."',
  declaration = 'I bring my honest desires to my Father, and I surrender to Him: not my will, but His be done.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You that Jesus prayed this prayer for me and drank the cup I deserved. Here is what I want. You know my heart. Nevertheless, not my will, but Yours be done. Give me grace to trust You. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy082' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Samuel was a boy serving in the house of God under the old priest Eli. The Bible says "the word of the LORD was precious in those days; there was no open vision." God was not speaking often. One night, God called Samuel by name. Three times the boy ran to Eli, thinking the old man had called him.

Finally Eli understood, and he taught Samuel what to say: "Speak, LORD; for thy servant heareth." When God called again, Samuel answered: "Speak; for thy servant heareth."

God was ready to speak. Samuel just needed to learn to recognise His voice and to listen.

Notice the order: "Speak, for your servant hears." Many of our prayers are the other way around: "Listen, Lord, for your servant is speaking." We bring long lists and leave no space for God to answer.

God speaks today mainly through His Word, as the Holy Spirit makes it alive in our hearts. He also speaks through wise counsel, circumstances and the quiet inner nudge of His Spirit. But we must slow down enough to listen.

Eli''s role is important too. Sometimes we need an older believer to help us recognise God''s voice.

Today: open your Bible to a short passage. Before you read, pray: "Speak, Lord, for Your servant is listening." Read slowly, then sit quietly for a few minutes and write down what stands out to you.',
  declaration = 'Speak, Lord, for Your servant is listening. I make room to hear God''s voice.',
  prayer = 'Lord, I want to hear You. Forgive me for doing all the talking. Teach me to recognise Your voice through Your Word and Your Spirit. Speak, Lord, for Your servant is listening. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy083' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Judah faced a powerful enemy, Assyria, and was tempted to run to Egypt for help: horses, chariots, military alliances. God spoke through Isaiah: "In returning and rest shall ye be saved; in quietness and in confidence shall be your strength."

Salvation would come not through frantic activity, but through returning to God and resting in Him. Strength would come not through noise and panic, but through quiet trust.

Then comes a sad line: "and ye would not." They refused. They said, "No; for we will flee upon horses." They trusted in speed and strength instead of God. And God said: then your pursuers will be swift too.

We often respond to crisis like Judah: run faster, work harder, make more calls, borrow more money, find another plan. Some action is wise and necessary. But when our activity comes from panic instead of trust, it leaves us exhausted and still afraid.

God''s way seems strange: return, rest, be quiet, be confident in Him. It feels too passive. But it is not weakness. It is the strength of a heart anchored in God.

Today: in the situation that is stressing you most, stop and ask: "Am I running to my own Egypt, or returning to God?" Spend some time quietly trusting Him before you take your next step.',
  declaration = 'In returning and rest I am saved. In quietness and confidence in God is my strength.',
  prayer = 'Lord, forgive me for running to my own solutions in panic. I return to You and rest in You. Give me a quiet, confident heart that trusts You in the middle of pressure. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy084' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'David wrote Psalm 62 while people were plotting against him, trying to bring him down from his position, speaking blessings with their mouths but cursing in their hearts. And yet the psalm is calm and steady. David says, "Truly my soul waiteth upon God: from him cometh my salvation."

Then he turns to the people and says: "Trust in him at all times; ye people, pour out your heart before him: God is a refuge for us. Selah."

"At all times" means in good times and bad times, in health and sickness, when we understand and when we do not.

"Pour out your heart" is a vivid picture. Think of turning a water jug upside down so everything flows out. God invites us to bring all of it: tears, anger, fear, confusion, hopes.

Many of us were taught to hide our feelings, especially in front of God. We think a "good Christian" should always say "I am fine". But the psalms show us people who poured out their hearts honestly and found God to be a refuge.

"Selah" probably meant a pause in the music. Stop and think about this.

Today: find a private place and pour out your heart to God. Say everything you are carrying. Then pause, Selah, and let Him be your refuge.',
  declaration = 'I trust God at all times. I pour out my heart before Him, and He is my refuge.',
  prayer = 'Lord, I trust You at all times, even when I do not understand. Here is my heart: everything I feel and carry. I pour it out before You. Be my refuge and my rock. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy085' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'The chapter begins by telling us that Jeremiah was "shut up in the court of the prison" when God spoke these words to him. Jerusalem was under siege by the Babylonians. Everything looked hopeless. Jeremiah himself was locked up for speaking God''s message.

And there, God said: "Call unto me, and I will answer thee, and shew thee great and mighty things, which thou knowest not."

The "great and mighty things" here included God''s promise that, after judgment, He would restore Jerusalem, heal the people and bring joy back to the city. From a prison cell, Jeremiah was shown a future he could not imagine.

Sometimes God speaks most clearly when we are most confined: in a hospital bed, a season of unemployment, a lockdown, a waiting period. We cannot move much, but we can still call on Him.

The promise is that He will answer and show us things we do not know. Not only answers to our questions, but a bigger view of His purposes.

We often limit God by what we already know. We pray for what seems possible. But God invites us to call on Him and let Him show us more.

Today: wherever you feel shut in, call on God. Ask Him to show you what you do not know about His plans for your situation, your family, your church and your nation.',
  declaration = 'I call to God, and He answers me. He shows me great and mighty things that I do not know.',
  prayer = 'Lord, I call to You from where I am, even if it feels like a prison. Answer me. Open my eyes to see beyond my situation. Show me great and mighty things You are doing that I do not yet know. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy086' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'In Isaiah 44, God mocks the idols people made. A man cuts down a tree, uses half of it to cook his food and warm himself, and carves the other half into a god and bows down to it. God says: how foolish to worship what your own hands made.

Then, in the middle of this, comes grace: "I have blotted out, as a thick cloud, thy transgressions, and, as a cloud, thy sins: return unto me; for I have redeemed thee."

Look at the order. God does not say, "Return to Me, and then I will blot out your sins." He says, "I have blotted out your sins... return to me." The forgiveness comes first, and it becomes the reason to come home.

Think of a thick morning cloud on the hills. When the sun rises and the wind blows, it disappears. You look up later and cannot even find where it was. That is what God does with the sins of those who come to Him.

"I have redeemed thee" means God has paid the price to buy you back. For us, that price was the blood of Jesus.

Many people stay away from God because they think they must first fix their lives. But God says the debt has been dealt with. Come home.

Today: if you have been staying away from God because of guilt, come back today. Thank Him that in Christ your sins are blotted out like a cloud.',
  declaration = 'God has blotted out my sins like a cloud. He has redeemed me, so I return to Him.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You that You have blotted out my sins like a thick cloud and redeemed me through Jesus. I will not stay away in shame. I return to You today with gratitude. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy087' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Mark describes a very busy day in Jesus'' ministry. He taught in the synagogue, cast out an unclean spirit, healed Peter''s mother-in-law, and that evening the whole city gathered at the door. He healed many sick people and cast out many demons.

Then the next morning: "rising up a great while before day, he went out, and departed into a solitary place, and there prayed."

After a day of great success and great demand, Jesus did not sleep late or rush into more activity. He went to be alone with His Father.

Soon the disciples found Him and said, "All men seek for thee." The crowds wanted more. But Jesus said, "Let us go into the next towns, that I may preach there also." His time in prayer gave Him clarity about His mission, even when the crowd had other plans.

If Jesus, the Son of God, needed time alone with the Father, how much more do we?

Many of us pray only when we have time left over. Jesus made time first. He did not let busyness steal His secret place.

The early morning may not suit everyone. Some people pray best at night or during lunch. What matters is that we make a real, regular, protected time for God.

Today: decide on a regular time and place to meet God this week. Guard it like an important appointment, because it is.',
  declaration = 'Like Jesus, I make time to be alone with my Father. In prayer I find strength and direction.',
  prayer = 'Lord Jesus, You made time to pray even when You were busy and in demand. Teach me to protect my time with the Father. Let prayer give me clarity about what matters most. In Your name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy088' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Psalm 25 is David''s prayer for guidance and forgiveness. In the middle of it, he says: "The meek will he guide in judgment: and the meek will he teach his way."

Meekness is often misunderstood. It is not weakness, shyness or allowing people to walk over you. Moses was described as very meek, and yet he confronted Pharaoh and led a nation. Jesus called Himself "meek and lowly in heart", and He drove the money changers out of the temple.

Meekness is strength under God''s control. It is a heart that is teachable, that does not insist on its own way, that bends to God''s will.

And this verse says God guides and teaches the meek. A proud person does not need a teacher; they think they already know. A meek person says, "Lord, show me. I am willing to learn and to change."

Think of a horse that is strong but trained. It has power, but it responds to the rider''s gentle touch. That is meekness.

As March ends, this is a good question to ask: Have I become more teachable this month? Have I let God correct, guide and reshape me, or have I resisted?

Today: ask God to make you meek: strong, but surrendered. Then think of one area where you have been resisting His guidance, and choose to bend.',
  declaration = 'God guides me in what is right and teaches me His way, because I choose a meek and teachable heart.',
  prayer = 'Lord, make me meek: strong but surrendered to You. Remove my stubbornness and pride. Guide me in what is right and teach me Your way, especially in the areas where I have resisted You. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy089' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'The book of Hosea is a love story about an unfaithful people and a faithful God. Through fourteen chapters, God describes Israel''s wandering and His pain. But the final chapter ends with an invitation to return and with these beautiful promises: "I will heal their backsliding, I will love them freely: for mine anger is turned away from him."

Backsliding is like slowly sliding down a hill. Nobody plans it. It often begins with small compromises: skipping prayer, neglecting the Word, tolerating a sin, drifting from fellowship.

God does not only forgive backsliding. He heals it. He goes to the root of what caused the drift and restores us.

"I will love them freely" means His love is not purchased. It is not reluctant. It is generous and overflowing.

The next verse says, "I will be as the dew unto Israel: he shall grow as the lily." In a dry land, dew brings life every morning. God promises to refresh His people so they blossom again.

Perhaps this month you have been honest with God about your distance, your pride or your coldness. Maybe you have felt like a backslider. Today''s word is good news: God heals and loves freely.

Today: thank God for the ways He has drawn you closer this month. Ask Him to heal any backsliding and to be like the dew to you, so your faith blossoms again.',
  declaration = 'God heals my backsliding and loves me freely. He is like the dew to me, and I will blossom again.',
  prayer = 'Lord, thank You for drawing me close this month. Heal every place where I have drifted. Love me freely, as You promised, and be like the morning dew to my soul. Let my faith blossom again. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy090' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;
